150: ## Standing design rules: section 0, the paid-for rules (spec section 4; standing-rules.md)
151: 
152: Six design rules this project has already paid for with reproduced defects. They
153: bind every phase. A spec discharges each by name (the specific loader that must
154: be total, the specific layer that gets a time-bound test, the specific policy
155: walk that must not sprout per-kind copies) or argues it inapplicable in one
156: sentence.
157: 
158: ### 1. Totality belongs to the function, not the caller
159: 
160: "This only ever sees validated documents" is a claim about today's callers.
161: Any new consumer of document shapes is **total over arbitrary JSON**, with a
162: hostile-input test. The defect that bought this rule: a gate that consumed
163: proposal-supplied bytes with no validation layer in front of it, in a codebase
164: where every other consumer had one.
165: 
166: Totality is more than catching one exception class. Check all of:
167: 
168: - The cap is applied to the **read**, not after it. A cap applied after
169:   `read_bytes()` is a report, not a cap.
170: - Exception classes that are not the obvious one: a very long integer literal
171:   raises `ValueError`, not `JSONDecodeError`; deep nesting raises
172:   `RecursionError`.
173: - The non-JSON constants. `NaN` and infinities are accepted by default and are
174:   not JSON.
175: - The docstring. If it promises totality, a traceback out of that function is a
176:   contradiction of the shipped contract, not a rough edge.
177: 
178: ### 2. An input cap bounds the document, not the algorithm
179: 
180: A rule quadratic in a count the document controls turns a legal input into
181: minutes of CPU under the cap. Every phase that adds work proportional to
182: untrusted input ships a **time-bound test over the layer it adds**. This is the
183: class that was caught once and that nobody searched for twice.
184: 
185: ### 3. Validation order is part of the design, stated per boundary
186: 
187: Which check runs first is a design decision with a user-visible consequence: a
188: gated delta should report "gated", not "your candidate is invalid", or the author
189: is sent into the other half of the same gate. Where two orderings both reject and
190: no return value distinguishes them, **pin the order by source-order assertion**.
191: 
192: ### 4. A policy governing N element kinds is enforced by one walk over all N
193: 
194: Not N copies of the policy. A shared policy implemented per-kind will be right in
195: the kinds someone remembered and absent in the rest. The corollary bites on the
196: fix side too: when **one** function serves both an authoring-time warning and an
197: evaluation-time verdict, a defect in it is wrong in both places and is fixed
198: once, which is the point.
199: 
200: ### 5. Normative prose has exactly one home
201: 
202: A document written alongside its implementation is a second chance to make the
203: same mistake, not independent evidence. Keep each normative sentence in one home
204: so a divergence is a **bug** rather than an ambiguity; everything else cites it.
205: When a phase's work makes two homes disagree, one of them yields in that phase,
206: and the spec names which.
207: 
208: **Scope:** the rule governs normative *prose*. It does not forbid byte-identical
209: sync-pinned copies of an interchange schema: a canonical reference plus a
210: vendored copy held identical by a mechanical identity test is ONE artifact. This
211: scope is written down because a reviewer reading the unscoped rule literally
212: reported such a pair as a violation.
213: 
214: ### 6. Resolve contested representation questions by measurement, not argument
215: 
216: Where the codebase can host an experiment, run it. A contested digest question
217: was settled by making the change and watching eight committed-payload tests go
218: red: the artifact's own contract decided it, in minutes, with no debate.
219: 
220: ## Failure classes this project has committed (standing-rules.md, second list)
221: 
222: These add to the classes the shared `standing-rules.md` lists.
223: 
224: - **A rule made unfalsifiable by how the fixtures were built.** A grading rule
225:   quantified over "any" versus "every" reads identically on fixtures where the
226:   subject is only ever touched once. The fixture set has to contain the
227:   discriminating case, or the rule ships unverified while its coverage row says
228:   otherwise. Ask of every rule: *what fixture would tell the two readings apart,
229:   and is it in the set?*
230: - **An interchange field defined in Python-only terms.** Every digest or
231:   identifier a **caller** must reproduce is defined over canonical JSON, with a
232:   stated empty case and a shipped producer. In-process markers may use
233:   language-specific representations only if they never cross a boundary.
234:   Related traps, each seen:
235:   - A digest that **coerces non-string mapping keys** loses content: two distinct
236:     documents hash identically. That is different in kind from a spelling
237:     coercion, and it is refused rather than documented.
238:   - A canonical-form helper **re-implemented** at a second site with the same
239:     arguments and no guarantee they stay the same arguments.
240:   - A digest taken over a section that something else then **writes into**: the
241:     digest moves and every committed artifact stops matching its own source.
242: - **Fixtures here are fixture models.** A finding class whose deontic half is
243:   unreachable with the fixture model is half-covered, and closing it may mean
244:   editing the fixture model rather than adding cases. An independence pass
245:   bundle carries the normative documents, the model, and enough data to author
246:   cases.
247: 
248: ### Implementation traps worth a checklist
249: 
250: Each of these was a real finding, and each generalizes past the phase that found
251: it.
252: 
253: - **The schema's spelling and the implementation's spelling differ.** Check the
254:   enum members literally, and assert the implementation's map covers the enum.
255: - **"Not implemented" folded into the same branch as "violated."** The failure
256:   mode is the bad one: input that *satisfies* a rule is reported as failing it.
257:   Raise a distinct unknown-operator error instead of fabricating a verdict.
258: - **A property decided about the wrong subject**: about the type rather than
259:   about the binding, so the answer depends on which fact happens to come first.
260: - **A guard that goes quiet exactly when it matters**: a fallback to a null
261:   state makes every later check look clean, losing the strong finding and
262:   reporting only the weak one.
263: - **First-match where a multimap was needed**: only the first eligible item
264:   compiles, or a repeated variable keeps only its last role, so a pattern
265:   licenses more than it checks.
266: - **A guard that assumes history.** "This is the only writer" does not entail
267:   "nothing removes it"; check both directions before compiling an obligation.
